1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,301
It is September, in the year 9 AD.

2
00:00:02,301 --> 00:00:05,618
You are twenty-six years old, soaked to the bone,

3
00:00:05,618 --> 00:00:09,611
and standing in a German forest so thick you cannot see the

4
00:00:09,611 --> 00:00:12,589
man three places ahead of you in the column.

5
00:00:12,589 --> 00:00:14,416
It has rained for two days.

6
00:00:14,416 --> 00:00:16,379
Your sandals are full of mud,

7
00:00:16,379 --> 00:00:18,680
your shield is twice as heavy wet,

8
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:22,674
and somewhere in the trees, thousands of men who used to be

9
00:00:22,674 --> 00:00:25,720
your allies are quietly moving into position.

10
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:27,344
By the end of this week,

11
00:00:27,344 --> 00:00:29,171
your legion will not exist.

12
00:00:29,171 --> 00:00:31,608
Its number will never be used again.

13
00:00:31,608 --> 00:00:33,300
This is how you got here.

14
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:34,815
Good evening.

15
00:00:34,815 --> 00:00:38,828
I'm the Archivero, and I keep the files nobody asked for.

16
00:00:38,828 --> 00:00:40,800
Tonight's file is a uniform.

17
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:45,235
Red tunic, iron helmet, hobnailed sandals: the Roman legionary,

18
00:00:45,235 --> 00:00:47,699
the most famous soldier in history.

19
00:00:47,699 --> 00:00:52,346
For about four hundred years, men like you held an empire together

20
00:00:52,346 --> 00:00:55,021
that stretched from Scotland to Syria.

21
00:00:55,021 --> 00:00:57,345
Not with genius generals, mostly.

22
00:00:57,345 --> 00:01:02,625
With walking, digging, paperwork, and a level of discipline that would make

23
00:01:02,625 --> 00:01:04,737
a modern drill sergeant faint.

24
00:01:04,737 --> 00:01:06,919
So let's put the helmet on you,

25
00:01:06,919 --> 00:01:08,750
and see how long you last.

26
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:12,005
Rewind eight years, to the year 1 AD.

27
00:01:12,005 --> 00:01:16,381
You are eighteen, the son of a poor farmer in northern Italy,

28
00:01:16,381 --> 00:01:20,471
and you have a problem: your older brother gets the farm,

29
00:01:20,471 --> 00:01:21,905
and you get nothing.

30
00:01:21,905 --> 00:01:26,067
A century earlier that would have kept you out of the army

31
00:01:26,067 --> 00:01:30,587
entirely, because Roman soldiers were supposed to own property.

32
00:01:30,587 --> 00:01:35,824
But after the reforms traditionally credited to the general Gaius Marius,

33
00:01:35,824 --> 00:01:38,407
the legions started taking the poor.

34
00:01:38,407 --> 00:01:40,201
Now the army is a career.

35
00:01:40,201 --> 00:01:42,927
Steady pay, a roof, three meals a day,

36
00:01:42,927 --> 00:01:46,012
and a big bonus at the end, if you survive.

37
00:01:46,012 --> 00:01:51,106
You walk to the recruiting officer with a letter of recommendation from

38
00:01:51,106 --> 00:01:53,187
a local big man, because yes,

39
00:01:53,187 --> 00:01:56,128
even in ancient Rome, it is who you know.

40
00:01:56,128 --> 00:01:58,137
You must be a Roman citizen,

41
00:01:58,137 --> 00:01:59,931
healthy, and tall enough.

42
00:01:59,931 --> 00:02:03,518
A doctor checks your eyes, your teeth, your limbs.

43
00:02:03,518 --> 00:02:04,164
You pass.

44
00:02:04,164 --> 00:02:08,181
They give you a small lead tag to wear around your neck,

45
00:02:08,181 --> 00:02:10,047
a kind of ancient dog tag,

46
00:02:10,047 --> 00:02:12,343
and you swear the military oath,

47
00:02:12,343 --> 00:02:15,212
the sacramentum: to obey your commander,

48
00:02:15,212 --> 00:02:18,943
to never desert, and to never abandon the standards.

49
00:02:18,943 --> 00:02:20,091
Congratulations.

50
00:02:20,091 --> 00:02:22,100
You belong to the state now.

51
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,665
Read the contract you did not get to read.

52
00:02:25,665 --> 00:02:30,466
Under the emperor Augustus, a legionary signed up for sixteen years,

53
00:02:30,466 --> 00:02:32,302
plus four more in reserve.

54
00:02:32,302 --> 00:02:35,267
In the year 5 AD that became twenty years,

55
00:02:35,267 --> 00:02:37,809
plus five more as a veteran on call.

56
00:02:37,809 --> 00:02:40,421
Later it would simply be twenty-five.

57
00:02:40,421 --> 00:02:42,821
You may not marry while you serve.

58
00:02:42,821 --> 00:02:44,728
If you already have a wife,

59
00:02:44,728 --> 00:02:47,693
as far as the law is concerned, you don't.

60
00:02:47,693 --> 00:02:52,070
Your pay is two hundred and twenty-five silver denarii a year.

61
00:02:52,070 --> 00:02:55,671
That sounds fine until the deductions arrive: food,

62
00:02:55,671 --> 00:03:00,330
boots, clothing, your tent, the burial club that will pay for your

63
00:03:00,330 --> 00:03:03,013
funeral, and a forced savings account.

64
00:03:03,013 --> 00:03:07,744
Some soldiers finished the year with almost nothing in their hands.

65
00:03:07,744 --> 00:03:10,144
And no, you were not paid in salt.

66
00:03:10,144 --> 00:03:14,874
The idea that "salary" comes from Roman soldiers being paid in salt

67
00:03:14,874 --> 00:03:16,075
is mostly a myth.

68
00:03:16,075 --> 00:03:17,698
You were paid in coins.

69
00:03:17,698 --> 00:03:18,475
Small ones.

70
00:03:19,075 --> 00:03:21,158
Now you learn to be a Roman.

71
00:03:21,158 --> 00:03:24,059
According to the Roman writer Vegetius,

72
00:03:24,059 --> 00:03:27,108
recruits had to march twenty Roman miles,

73
00:03:27,108 --> 00:03:32,241
around eighteen of ours, in five summer hours at the regular military

74
00:03:32,241 --> 00:03:35,141
pace, and twenty-four at the fast pace.

75
00:03:35,141 --> 00:03:38,861
While carrying a load of about sixty Roman pounds.

76
00:03:38,861 --> 00:03:40,497
You train twice a day.

77
00:03:40,497 --> 00:03:44,662
You fight a wooden post with a wooden sword and a wicker

78
00:03:44,662 --> 00:03:48,456
shield, both made twice as heavy as the real thing,

79
00:03:48,456 --> 00:03:50,538
so the real ones feel light.

80
00:03:50,538 --> 00:03:52,844
You learn to stab, never slash,

81
00:03:52,844 --> 00:03:56,712
because a stab kills and a slash just annoys people.

82
00:03:56,712 --> 00:03:58,869
You learn to throw a javelin,

83
00:03:58,869 --> 00:04:01,844
to swim, to jump, and above all, to dig.

84
00:04:01,844 --> 00:04:06,382
Because the secret weapon of the Roman army is not the sword.

85
00:04:06,382 --> 00:04:07,646
It is the shovel.

86
00:04:07,646 --> 00:04:13,076
Every single night on campaign, the entire legion builds a fortified camp

87
00:04:13,076 --> 00:04:16,051
from scratch: a ditch, an earth rampart,

88
00:04:16,051 --> 00:04:20,440
a palisade of stakes, and neat streets of tents in the same

89
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:25,200
layout every time, so that every man knows exactly where his bed

90
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:28,250
is, in the dark, in any country on earth.

91
00:04:28,850 --> 00:04:30,019
Let's dress you.

92
00:04:30,019 --> 00:04:34,986
A wool tunic, a cloak, and caligae: heavy sandals with iron hobnails

93
00:04:34,986 --> 00:04:39,952
in the soles, which grip the road and announce you from a mile away.

94
00:04:39,952 --> 00:04:42,217
A helmet with big cheek guards.

95
00:04:42,217 --> 00:04:44,700
Body armour: a shirt of iron mail,

96
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:49,010
or the famous plate armour made of overlapping iron strips.

97
00:04:49,010 --> 00:04:51,931
A curved rectangular shield, the scutum,

98
00:04:51,931 --> 00:04:53,976
about as tall as your chest.

99
00:04:53,976 --> 00:04:58,797
Two javelins, the pila, with long soft iron necks designed to bend

100
00:04:58,797 --> 00:05:02,522
when they hit, so the enemy cannot throw them back,

101
00:05:02,522 --> 00:05:06,978
and a shield with a bent javelin stuck in it becomes useless.

102
00:05:06,978 --> 00:05:07,635
A dagger.

103
00:05:07,635 --> 00:05:12,383
And the gladius, a short sword built for stabbing at close range.

104
00:05:12,383 --> 00:05:13,625
Then the luggage.

105
00:05:13,625 --> 00:05:18,080
On campaign you carry a pole over your shoulder loaded with a

106
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:20,271
cooking pot, a saw, a pickaxe,

107
00:05:20,271 --> 00:05:22,755
a basket for moving earth, a rope,

108
00:05:22,755 --> 00:05:25,311
a sickle, and several days of food.

109
00:05:25,311 --> 00:05:30,132
The Jewish historian Josephus, who watched Roman legions up close,

110
00:05:30,132 --> 00:05:32,104
listed almost exactly that.

111
00:05:32,104 --> 00:05:36,925
Soldiers carried so much that they got a nickname: Marius's mules.

112
00:05:37,525 --> 00:05:38,805
You are not alone.

113
00:05:38,805 --> 00:05:42,857
You are one of eight men in a contubernium: a tent group.

114
00:05:42,857 --> 00:05:46,838
Eight men, one leather tent, one mule, one cooking fire.

115
00:05:46,838 --> 00:05:49,823
These seven strangers are now your family,

116
00:05:49,823 --> 00:05:54,231
your kitchen, and the people who will be ordered to punish you

117
00:05:54,231 --> 00:05:55,439
if you fail them.

118
00:05:55,439 --> 00:05:58,496
Ten tent groups make a century: eighty men,

119
00:05:58,496 --> 00:06:00,273
commanded by a centurion.

120
00:06:00,273 --> 00:06:02,264
Six centuries make a cohort.

121
00:06:02,264 --> 00:06:05,960
Ten cohorts make a legion, around five thousand men,

122
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:08,875
with the first cohort at double strength.

123
00:06:08,875 --> 00:06:11,861
At the top of the legion stands its eagle,

124
00:06:11,861 --> 00:06:15,984
the aquila, a golden eagle on a pole, carried into battle.

125
00:06:15,984 --> 00:06:18,543
The eagle is the soul of the legion.

126
00:06:18,543 --> 00:06:22,524
Losing it is the worst disgrace a Roman unit can suffer.

127
00:06:22,524 --> 00:06:25,367
Remember that, because it matters later.

128
00:06:25,367 --> 00:06:27,500
Your legion is the Eighteenth.

129
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:32,696
On campaign, every day starts before sunrise with three trumpet calls.

130
00:06:32,696 --> 00:06:35,125
At the first, you take down the tent.

131
00:06:35,125 --> 00:06:38,473
At the second, you load the mule and your own pole.

132
00:06:38,473 --> 00:06:40,771
At the third, the column moves out,

133
00:06:40,771 --> 00:06:42,872
and woe to anyone still packing.

134
00:06:42,872 --> 00:06:46,943
The order of march is always the same: scouts and light troops

135
00:06:46,943 --> 00:06:51,539
in front, then the surveyors and engineers who will mark out tonight's

136
00:06:51,539 --> 00:06:54,559
camp, then the general's baggage, the cavalry,

137
00:06:54,559 --> 00:06:58,498
the siege engines, the legions in long columns six men wide,

138
00:06:58,498 --> 00:07:00,796
and the camp followers at the back.

139
00:07:00,796 --> 00:07:02,897
You march for five or six hours,

140
00:07:02,897 --> 00:07:05,720
singing rude songs about your own generals,

141
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:08,149
which Roman soldiers were famous for.

142
00:07:08,149 --> 00:07:12,614
In the early afternoon you reach the spot the surveyors have chosen,

143
00:07:12,614 --> 00:07:17,472
and instead of resting, you put down your load and start digging the camp.

144
00:07:17,472 --> 00:07:22,396
By evening the legion is inside a fortress that did not exist that morning.

145
00:07:22,396 --> 00:07:25,876
You eat, you stand guard in shifts through the night,

146
00:07:25,876 --> 00:07:28,371
and tomorrow you burn it, or leave it,

147
00:07:28,371 --> 00:07:30,275
and do the whole thing again.

148
00:07:30,875 --> 00:07:33,052
Look at a map of your new world.

149
00:07:33,052 --> 00:07:36,589
Under Augustus there are about twenty-eight legions,

150
00:07:36,589 --> 00:07:40,195
around a hundred and fifty thousand citizen soldiers,

151
00:07:40,195 --> 00:07:44,277
plus roughly as many again in the auxiliary units made up of

152
00:07:44,277 --> 00:07:46,453
non-citizens from the provinces.

153
00:07:46,453 --> 00:07:50,535
That sounds like a lot until you see the size of the empire.

154
00:07:50,535 --> 00:07:53,120
Almost none of these men are in Italy.

155
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:55,297
They sit on the edges: in Spain,

156
00:07:55,297 --> 00:07:57,678
in the Balkans, in Syria, in Egypt,

157
00:07:57,678 --> 00:08:01,080
and above all along the great rivers in the north,

158
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:02,780
the Rhine and the Danube.

159
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:06,182
Beyond the Rhine lies Germania, a land of forests,

160
00:08:06,182 --> 00:08:11,692
marshes and dozens of tribes, which Augustus has decided to turn into a province.

161
00:08:11,692 --> 00:08:16,046
Your legion, the Eighteenth, is stationed on the Rhine frontier.

162
00:08:16,046 --> 00:08:18,495
Your job, for the next twenty years,

163
00:08:18,495 --> 00:08:19,719
is to be the wall.

164
00:08:19,719 --> 00:08:20,876
Not a stone wall.

165
00:08:20,876 --> 00:08:23,597
A wall made of men who are always there,

166
00:08:23,597 --> 00:08:26,250
always watching, always ready to march.

167
00:08:26,250 --> 00:08:29,515
The Romans would build actual stone walls later,

168
00:08:29,515 --> 00:08:31,624
like Hadrian's Wall in Britain.

169
00:08:31,624 --> 00:08:33,325
For now, the wall is you.

170
00:08:33,925 --> 00:08:36,752
Most of a legionary's life is not battle.

171
00:08:36,752 --> 00:08:37,648
It is chores.

172
00:08:37,648 --> 00:08:41,784
In winter quarters or in a permanent fort you do guard duty,

173
00:08:41,784 --> 00:08:45,783
cleaning, road building, bridge building, quarrying stone,

174
00:08:45,783 --> 00:08:50,816
cutting firewood, and paperwork, because the Roman army ran on paperwork.

175
00:08:50,816 --> 00:08:53,573
Letters found at the fort of Vindolanda,

176
00:08:53,573 --> 00:08:57,779
near Hadrian's Wall, written about a century after your time,

177
00:08:57,779 --> 00:09:00,054
show what soldiers worried about.

178
00:09:00,054 --> 00:09:03,156
One man was sent socks, two pairs of sandals,

179
00:09:03,156 --> 00:09:05,086
and two pairs of underpants.

180
00:09:05,086 --> 00:09:10,188
Another letter is a birthday party invitation between two officers' wives.

181
00:09:10,188 --> 00:09:11,980
You eat a ration of wheat,

182
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:14,324
which you grind and bake yourself,

183
00:09:14,324 --> 00:09:17,427
plus whatever your tent group can add: bacon,

184
00:09:17,427 --> 00:09:22,046
cheese, lentils, vegetables, and posca, sour wine mixed with water.

185
00:09:22,046 --> 00:09:24,735
On the march you eat hard, dry biscuit.

186
00:09:24,735 --> 00:09:26,182
You gamble with dice.

187
00:09:26,182 --> 00:09:27,906
You go to the bath house,

188
00:09:27,906 --> 00:09:30,388
because a proper Roman fort has one.

189
00:09:30,388 --> 00:09:31,560
And you complain.

190
00:09:31,560 --> 00:09:33,766
Every army in history complains.

191
00:09:33,766 --> 00:09:34,800
Yours does too.

192
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:37,632
Not everyone stays at the bottom.

193
00:09:37,632 --> 00:09:39,661
If you can read, write, count,

194
00:09:39,661 --> 00:09:44,531
or do anything useful, you might become an immunis: a specialist excused

195
00:09:44,531 --> 00:09:46,018
from the worst chores.

196
00:09:46,018 --> 00:09:50,077
Surveyors, engineers, clerks, medics, blacksmiths, butchers,

197
00:09:50,077 --> 00:09:52,782
even the men who look after the weapons.

198
00:09:52,782 --> 00:09:58,193
Above them are the principales, junior officers on pay and a half or double pay.

199
00:09:58,193 --> 00:10:00,627
The optio is the centurion's deputy.

200
00:10:00,627 --> 00:10:05,091
The tesserarius runs the guard and hands out the nightly password,

201
00:10:05,091 --> 00:10:06,917
written on a little tablet.

202
00:10:06,917 --> 00:10:11,922
The signifer carries the century's standard and also looks after the men's

203
00:10:11,922 --> 00:10:16,792
savings, which makes him a mix between a flag bearer and a bank manager.

204
00:10:16,792 --> 00:10:20,038
The aquilifer carries the legion's eagle itself,

205
00:10:20,038 --> 00:10:23,623
the most honoured and most dangerous job in the unit.

206
00:10:23,623 --> 00:10:25,990
And above them all, the centurions,

207
00:10:25,990 --> 00:10:28,019
paid many times more than you,

208
00:10:28,019 --> 00:10:32,483
with their crests worn sideways across the helmet so their men can

209
00:10:32,483 --> 00:10:34,038
find them in the chaos.

210
00:10:34,038 --> 00:10:38,773
A few ordinary soldiers really did rise to centurion after many years.

211
00:10:38,773 --> 00:10:39,652
Most did not.

212
00:10:39,652 --> 00:10:42,425
You, for now, are scrubbing the latrines.

213
00:10:43,025 --> 00:10:47,081
Outside the walls of every big fortress grows a second town,

214
00:10:47,081 --> 00:10:51,407
the canabae: taverns, shops, craftsmen, merchants, and families.

215
00:10:51,407 --> 00:10:56,950
Because soldiers cannot legally marry, thousands of them simply live with partners

216
00:10:56,950 --> 00:11:01,005
there anyway, and have children the law pretends not to see.

217
00:11:01,005 --> 00:11:02,898
This is where your pay goes.

218
00:11:02,898 --> 00:11:05,399
Wine, better food, dice, a new cloak,

219
00:11:05,399 --> 00:11:10,536
a good-luck charm, a letter written for you by a scribe if you cannot write.

220
00:11:10,536 --> 00:11:13,510
Soldiers saved money, lent it to each other,

221
00:11:13,510 --> 00:11:14,930
and lost it gambling.

222
00:11:14,930 --> 00:11:16,890
Some had slaves of their own.

223
00:11:16,890 --> 00:11:20,743
And all of them listened for news of a donative: an extra

224
00:11:20,743 --> 00:11:24,596
cash gift from the emperor, usually at the start of a new

225
00:11:24,596 --> 00:11:28,719
reign or after a victory, which could be worth months of pay.

226
00:11:28,719 --> 00:11:31,490
Life in a Roman fort was hard and boring,

227
00:11:31,490 --> 00:11:34,667
but it was also oddly comfortable for the time.

228
00:11:34,667 --> 00:11:38,655
Regular food, a doctor, a bath house and a salary were more

229
00:11:38,655 --> 00:11:41,900
than most farmers in the empire would ever have.

230
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:44,239
The army is also a church.

231
00:11:44,239 --> 00:11:48,585
In the middle of every fortress stands the headquarters building,

232
00:11:48,585 --> 00:11:52,531
and in its heart is a shrine holding the legion's eagle and

233
00:11:52,531 --> 00:11:55,005
the standards, guarded day and night.

234
00:11:55,005 --> 00:12:00,221
Beneath the shrine, very conveniently, sits the strongroom with the pay chest,

235
00:12:00,221 --> 00:12:02,294
so if you want to rob the army,

236
00:12:02,294 --> 00:12:04,367
you have to rob the gods first.

237
00:12:04,367 --> 00:12:07,443
Every year you renew your oath to the emperor.

238
00:12:07,443 --> 00:12:11,722
The army keeps a calendar of festivals for the emperor's family,

239
00:12:11,722 --> 00:12:15,735
for Mars, for Jupiter, for the birthday of the eagle itself.

240
00:12:15,735 --> 00:12:18,075
Soldiers also bring their own gods.

241
00:12:18,075 --> 00:12:22,221
A century or two later, many legionaries will worship Mithras,

242
00:12:22,221 --> 00:12:26,902
a god of light whose followers meet in small dark underground temples,

243
00:12:26,902 --> 00:12:28,908
with secret ranks like a club.

244
00:12:28,908 --> 00:12:32,720
You touch your lucky charm, you pour a little wine on the

245
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:36,732
ground for the gods, and you hope they are paying attention.

246
00:12:36,732 --> 00:12:39,875
In a few years you will find out they were not.

247
00:12:40,475 --> 00:12:44,263
Quick question before your legion marches into Germany.

248
00:12:44,263 --> 00:12:47,224
Out of everything so far, the twenty years,

249
00:12:47,224 --> 00:12:50,185
the marching, the digging, the no marriage,

250
00:12:50,185 --> 00:12:53,973
the underpants letters, what would make you quit first?

251
00:12:53,973 --> 00:12:55,626
Tell me in the comments.

252
00:12:55,626 --> 00:12:58,725
I'll read the worst answers in the next file.

253
00:12:59,325 --> 00:13:02,778
You will not quit, because quitting is desertion,

254
00:13:02,778 --> 00:13:05,245
and Roman discipline is terrifying.

255
00:13:05,245 --> 00:13:07,782
Your centurion carries a vine stick,

256
00:13:07,782 --> 00:13:10,178
the vitis, the symbol of his rank,

257
00:13:10,178 --> 00:13:12,152
and he uses it on your back.

258
00:13:12,152 --> 00:13:16,380
The historian Tacitus describes a centurion called Lucilius,

259
00:13:16,380 --> 00:13:19,199
nicknamed by his men "Bring me another",

260
00:13:19,199 --> 00:13:23,498
because he broke his stick over so many soldiers that he kept

261
00:13:23,498 --> 00:13:25,119
shouting for a new one.

262
00:13:25,119 --> 00:13:27,868
When his men mutinied, they killed him.

263
00:13:27,868 --> 00:13:32,449
Fall asleep on night guard, and you may face the fustuarium: your

264
00:13:32,449 --> 00:13:35,268
own comrades beat or stone you to death.

265
00:13:35,268 --> 00:13:38,087
And if a whole unit runs away in battle,

266
00:13:38,087 --> 00:13:42,950
the commander can order a decimation: the unit is divided into groups

267
00:13:42,950 --> 00:13:46,896
of ten, lots are drawn, and in each group the other nine

268
00:13:46,896 --> 00:13:48,447
kill the one who lost.

269
00:13:48,447 --> 00:13:50,138
It was rare, but famous.

270
00:13:50,138 --> 00:13:53,169
In 71 BC, during the war against Spartacus,

271
00:13:53,169 --> 00:13:55,001
Marcus Crassus revived it.

272
00:13:55,001 --> 00:13:58,525
Nobody in your century plans to be the one in ten.

273
00:13:59,125 --> 00:14:03,587
Before your first battle, the good news: the Roman army has some

274
00:14:03,587 --> 00:14:06,514
of the best medicine in the ancient world.

275
00:14:06,514 --> 00:14:09,721
Big forts have a hospital, the valetudinarium,

276
00:14:09,721 --> 00:14:12,858
with wards of small rooms around a courtyard.

277
00:14:12,858 --> 00:14:17,947
There are doctors, and orderlies who carry bandages onto the battlefield.

278
00:14:17,947 --> 00:14:21,433
Archaeologists have found Roman scalpels, forceps,

279
00:14:21,433 --> 00:14:27,219
probes, bone levers and needles, many of them surprisingly similar to modern tools.

280
00:14:27,219 --> 00:14:30,565
Surgeons could pull out arrowheads, stitch cuts,

281
00:14:30,565 --> 00:14:32,726
set broken bones, and amputate.

282
00:14:32,726 --> 00:14:35,445
The bad news: there are no antibiotics,

283
00:14:35,445 --> 00:14:38,512
no anaesthesia beyond strong wine and herbs,

284
00:14:38,512 --> 00:14:40,743
and nobody knows what germs are.

285
00:14:40,743 --> 00:14:42,834
A clean stab wound might heal.

286
00:14:42,834 --> 00:14:47,087
A dirty one in your belly almost certainly kills you, slowly.

287
00:14:47,087 --> 00:14:50,363
So most soldiers died not from a famous battle,

288
00:14:50,363 --> 00:14:54,406
but from fevers, dysentery, infected wounds and accidents.

289
00:14:54,406 --> 00:14:58,728
The enemy you should fear most is not a barbarian with an axe.

290
00:14:58,728 --> 00:15:00,750
It is the water in your camp.

291
00:15:01,350 --> 00:15:04,690
Your first real fight comes on a frontier river,

292
00:15:04,690 --> 00:15:06,360
against a raiding party.

293
00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:08,517
It is nothing like the stories.

294
00:15:08,517 --> 00:15:11,230
The noise comes first: horns, shouting,

295
00:15:11,230 --> 00:15:13,666
the drumming of weapons on shields.

296
00:15:13,666 --> 00:15:14,709
Then the order.

297
00:15:14,709 --> 00:15:17,980
You throw your first javelin, then your second,

298
00:15:17,980 --> 00:15:20,485
into the mass of men running at you.

299
00:15:20,485 --> 00:15:22,850
The iron necks bend, shields drop,

300
00:15:22,850 --> 00:15:24,451
and then the lines hit.

301
00:15:24,451 --> 00:15:28,208
You do not swing your sword like a hero in a painting.

302
00:15:28,208 --> 00:15:32,661
You crouch behind your shield, punch forward with its iron boss,

303
00:15:32,661 --> 00:15:35,653
and stab low, under the other man's shield,

304
00:15:35,653 --> 00:15:38,924
over and over, while the man behind you pushes,

305
00:15:38,924 --> 00:15:42,124
and the man beside you covers your right side.

306
00:15:42,124 --> 00:15:45,395
A Roman battle line is a machine made of tired,

307
00:15:45,395 --> 00:15:47,969
terrified people trusting each other.

308
00:15:47,969 --> 00:15:50,126
When the enemy breaks and runs,

309
00:15:50,126 --> 00:15:52,283
you are too exhausted to cheer.

310
00:15:52,283 --> 00:15:54,092
That night you dig a camp.

311
00:15:54,092 --> 00:15:55,275
Of course you do.

312
00:15:55,875 --> 00:15:58,123
The Roman army had another face,

313
00:15:58,123 --> 00:15:59,879
the one you see in films.

314
00:15:59,879 --> 00:16:04,936
The testudo, the tortoise: a block of men locking their shields together

315
00:16:04,936 --> 00:16:07,395
on every side and over their heads,

316
00:16:07,395 --> 00:16:10,907
so they can walk right up to an enemy wall under a

317
00:16:10,907 --> 00:16:12,733
rain of arrows and stones.

318
00:16:12,733 --> 00:16:17,580
The siege engines: the ballista, a giant crossbow that throws stones,

319
00:16:17,580 --> 00:16:22,497
and the scorpio, a smaller bolt thrower so accurate that Roman writers

320
00:16:22,497 --> 00:16:25,658
boast about it pinning enemies to the ground.

321
00:16:25,658 --> 00:16:30,154
And the most Roman thing of all: when Julius Caesar besieged the

322
00:16:30,154 --> 00:16:35,141
Gallic leader Vercingetorix at Alesia, half a century before your time,

323
00:16:35,141 --> 00:16:36,967
he did not storm the town.

324
00:16:36,967 --> 00:16:38,724
He built a ring of walls,

325
00:16:38,724 --> 00:16:41,604
ditches and towers all the way around it,

326
00:16:41,604 --> 00:16:44,343
and then a second ring facing outwards,

327
00:16:44,343 --> 00:16:47,434
against the relief army coming to rescue it.

328
00:16:47,434 --> 00:16:49,892
His men fought both armies at once,

329
00:16:49,892 --> 00:16:53,123
from inside their own fortifications, and won.

330
00:16:53,123 --> 00:16:55,863
Your instructors tell that story a lot.

331
00:16:55,863 --> 00:16:57,900
It mainly means more digging.

332
00:16:58,500 --> 00:17:02,465
In the spring of 9 AD your three legions cross the Rhine

333
00:17:02,465 --> 00:17:05,580
and march deep into Germania for the summer.

334
00:17:05,580 --> 00:17:11,881
Your general, Publius Quinctilius Varus, behaves as if the province is already conquered.

335
00:17:11,881 --> 00:17:14,996
He holds courts, hands down Roman judgments,

336
00:17:14,996 --> 00:17:19,598
and collects taxes from tribes who are not used to paying anyone.

337
00:17:19,598 --> 00:17:22,571
You spend the summer near the river Weser,

338
00:17:22,571 --> 00:17:25,190
building, patrolling and being bored.

339
00:17:25,190 --> 00:17:29,438
The local Germanic leaders smile, trade and dine with Varus.

340
00:17:29,438 --> 00:17:31,987
The most trusted of all is Arminius,

341
00:17:31,987 --> 00:17:34,182
a prince of the Cherusci tribe,

342
00:17:34,182 --> 00:17:38,005
who grew up in Rome, served as an officer in the Roman

343
00:17:38,005 --> 00:17:40,341
army and holds Roman citizenship.

344
00:17:40,341 --> 00:17:45,934
Another German noble, Segestes, warns Varus that Arminius is planning a revolt.

345
00:17:45,934 --> 00:17:49,403
Varus thinks it is a family feud and ignores him.

346
00:17:49,403 --> 00:17:51,173
At the end of the summer,

347
00:17:51,173 --> 00:17:55,350
the army starts back towards its winter camps on the Rhine.

348
00:17:55,950 --> 00:17:58,157
And now we're back in the rain.

349
00:17:58,157 --> 00:18:02,427
On the way home, news arrives of an uprising among a distant

350
00:18:02,427 --> 00:18:07,410
tribe, and Arminius suggests the army make a short detour to crush it.

351
00:18:07,410 --> 00:18:08,335
Varus agrees.

352
00:18:08,335 --> 00:18:12,961
Arminius rides ahead, saying he will gather his warriors to help.

353
00:18:12,961 --> 00:18:14,385
He does gather them.

354
00:18:14,385 --> 00:18:15,595
Just not to help.

355
00:18:15,595 --> 00:18:18,727
The detour leads your column, three legions,

356
00:18:18,727 --> 00:18:23,425
auxiliaries, cavalry, wagons, mules, servants, women and children,

357
00:18:23,425 --> 00:18:28,336
stretched out for miles, into a strip of land squeezed between wooded

358
00:18:28,336 --> 00:18:34,243
hills and a great bog, near what the Romans would remember as the Teutoburg Forest.

359
00:18:34,243 --> 00:18:36,877
Then the trees start throwing spears.

360
00:18:36,877 --> 00:18:40,934
For three or four days the column is attacked from cover,

361
00:18:40,934 --> 00:18:45,062
in mud and rain, in terrain where you cannot form a battle

362
00:18:45,062 --> 00:18:47,411
line, cannot build a proper camp,

363
00:18:47,411 --> 00:18:49,831
and cannot see who is killing you.

364
00:18:49,831 --> 00:18:55,241
The tribes Arminius has secretly united pick the army apart, piece by piece.

365
00:18:55,241 --> 00:18:59,725
Varus, wounded, falls on his own sword rather than be captured.

366
00:18:59,725 --> 00:19:02,359
Almost the entire force is destroyed,

367
00:19:02,359 --> 00:19:05,989
somewhere around fifteen to twenty thousand people.

368
00:19:05,989 --> 00:19:07,483
The eagles are taken.

369
00:19:07,483 --> 00:19:12,395
Two thousand years later, archaeologists at a place called Kalkriese,

370
00:19:12,395 --> 00:19:14,815
near the modern city of Osnabrück,

371
00:19:14,815 --> 00:19:18,658
found thousands of Roman objects in the ground: coins,

372
00:19:18,658 --> 00:19:22,217
weapons, armour, hobnails from sandals like yours,

373
00:19:22,217 --> 00:19:25,420
and the iron face mask of a Roman cavalryman.

374
00:19:25,420 --> 00:19:29,335
One of your officers, a centurion named Marcus Caelius,

375
00:19:29,335 --> 00:19:32,751
has a tombstone that still survives in a museum.

376
00:19:32,751 --> 00:19:35,314
It says he fell in the war of Varus,

377
00:19:35,314 --> 00:19:39,086
and that his bones may one day be placed in the tomb.

378
00:19:39,086 --> 00:19:40,225
They never were.

379
00:19:40,825 --> 00:19:45,291
In Rome, the old emperor Augustus takes the news so badly that,

380
00:19:45,291 --> 00:19:50,183
according to the historian Suetonius, he lets his hair and beard grow

381
00:19:50,183 --> 00:19:53,374
for months and bangs his head against a door,

382
00:19:53,374 --> 00:19:58,904
shouting: "Quinctilius Varus, give me back my legions!" The numbers Seventeen,

383
00:19:58,904 --> 00:20:03,299
Eighteen and Nineteen are never used for a Roman legion again.

384
00:20:03,299 --> 00:20:08,546
Six years later, a Roman army under Germanicus reaches the battlefield and

385
00:20:08,546 --> 00:20:10,956
finds the bones still lying there,

386
00:20:10,956 --> 00:20:14,288
skulls nailed to trees, and buries what it can.

387
00:20:14,288 --> 00:20:18,542
Two of the lost eagles are recovered in the following years,

388
00:20:18,542 --> 00:20:20,740
and the last one only in 41 AD,

389
00:20:20,740 --> 00:20:23,717
more than thirty years after it was taken.

390
00:20:23,717 --> 00:20:26,908
Rome never conquers Germany beyond the Rhine.

391
00:20:26,908 --> 00:20:30,665
A single ambush in the rain helps draw a border that,

392
00:20:30,665 --> 00:20:35,061
in a way, Europe still feels today: the line between the Latin

393
00:20:35,061 --> 00:20:36,975
world and the Germanic one.

394
00:20:37,575 --> 00:20:39,100
But let's be generous.

395
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:43,051
Say you were transferred to another legion a year before.

396
00:20:43,051 --> 00:20:44,437
What happens to you?

397
00:20:44,437 --> 00:20:47,626
If disease, accidents or battle don't get you,

398
00:20:47,626 --> 00:20:51,369
and many soldiers did die long before their discharge,

399
00:20:51,369 --> 00:20:54,973
you serve your twenty years plus your reserve years.

400
00:20:54,973 --> 00:20:58,855
Then comes the honourable discharge, the honesta missio.

401
00:20:58,855 --> 00:21:04,123
Under Augustus a retiring legionary received a bonus of about three thousand

402
00:21:04,123 --> 00:21:07,381
denarii, more than thirteen years of basic pay,

403
00:21:07,381 --> 00:21:10,153
or a plot of land in a veterans' colony.

404
00:21:10,153 --> 00:21:12,441
You would be in your mid-forties,

405
00:21:12,441 --> 00:21:14,659
with a bad back, iron-hard feet,

406
00:21:14,659 --> 00:21:17,431
and a family the law finally recognises.

407
00:21:17,431 --> 00:21:21,244
Many veterans settled near the forts where they served,

408
00:21:21,244 --> 00:21:25,195
married local women, and their sons joined the same army.

409
00:21:25,195 --> 00:21:30,185
That is how Rome turned frontier camps into cities: places like Cologne,

410
00:21:30,185 --> 00:21:32,542
York, and many others started out,

411
00:21:32,542 --> 00:21:35,800
in one way or another, as Roman military sites.

412
00:21:36,400 --> 00:21:37,788
So that's your file.

413
00:21:37,788 --> 00:21:41,049
Twenty-five years of marching, digging, chores,

414
00:21:41,049 --> 00:21:45,420
terrifying discipline and the occasional nightmare in a forest,

415
00:21:45,420 --> 00:21:47,779
for a pension and a piece of land.

416
00:21:47,779 --> 00:21:50,762
Most legionaries never saw a famous battle.

417
00:21:50,762 --> 00:21:55,550
They built roads and walls that are still standing two thousand years

418
00:21:55,550 --> 00:21:59,158
later, which is more than most of us will be able to

419
00:21:59,158 --> 00:22:00,476
say about our jobs.

420
00:22:00,476 --> 00:22:02,904
Next time, we open another uniform.

421
00:22:02,904 --> 00:22:04,500
I'll leave the lamp on.

